\section{Conclusion}
A flow language model's action is a path of noise coordinates, not a sequence of decisions, and the score-function estimator that powers AR post-training averages every reward bit over five orders of magnitude more nuisance than it was designed for. The differentiable chain offers the pathwise estimator instead, and \jpc{} makes it work with three ingredients whose necessity we measured: a two-dimensional critic over flow steps and token positions that pools to a value, joint training on the policy's own rollouts, and training under the deployment sampler with early stopping at the held-out peak. On four verifiable tasks the recipe is the only one that replicates across seeds, with its largest gain where supervised training cannot help; its verified samples then amortise into cheap samplers, and the controls show that this needs the RL policy. The limits are equally concrete: the loop is null when solutions are unique and cheap samplers already match the deployment one, and coverage-bound where the base already solves what the policy solves. We expect the estimator argument, not the architecture, to carry over to larger continuous-state models.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All results are for one 105M-parameter architecture on four synthetic tasks with exact verifiers; reward models, open-ended text and larger models are untested. \jpc{} needs a held-out set to choose the stopping point, because every run eventually collapses when the actor outruns the critic (a principled stopping rule or an actor trust region is the obvious next step); and the loop's gains depend on what verified samples add to the base's coverage.
